onna happen right  that's simply not going to happen so  those are the paths to reunification  that North Korea has referred to  favorably in the past for South Korea  and for most of the developed world  reunification of South Korea is more of  Korea is likely to be the Kim regime  relinquishing power how you work out the  logistics of that at this point I don't  know and North Korea becoming sort of  part of South Korea in a sense and  that's what's really concerning about  all of this I obviously hope for the  best this has been one of the worst  humanitarian crises  in a very very long time which we've  been covering for years but historically  the Kim regime has made similar  statements and claims and so-called  agreements that have gone nowhere  because they have because of how they  define a lot of these terms the theme  has been agreeing to do things that are  difficult to monitor demanding things  that are basically non-starters for  other countries to do and we're  definitely seeing some of that here it's  a good opportunity to restate that we've  learned that North Korea's nuclear  testing facility was hugely damaged by  an earthquake we still don't know the  full extent of that but if it's true  that would be a really good reason for  Kim jong-un to say let's agree to shut  down something that just broke and see  what political benefit we can get from  it and maybe we could even get other  countries to help us with the cleanup or  dealing with whatever Fallout and  radiation might be happening as a  consequence from those from that  potentially a hugely damaged nuclear  testing site as far as who gets credit  for this and this has been a big topic  of conversation my sense is that it's  mostly South Korea we actually don't  know at this point if China has been  involved behind the scenes we know that  Chinese President Xi has made it clear  that China isn't going to tolerate North  Korean nonsense any longer it's possible  that that's something that's go